From 9fd1e051360913d8e1ecd9712bf079fb8bfeac7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Maestro <jjmaestro@ieee.org>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 18:08:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] patch: compile with -std=gnu11 rather than -std=c11

`-std=c11` selects strict ISO C, which defines `__STRICT_ANSI__`. glibc reads
that as a request to expose the ISO C library only, and hides the POSIX and
GNU declarations that are visible by default. PostgreSQL is configured for the
GNU dialect and its server headers are written against it, so an extension
compiled in strict mode sees a different libc than the server it is being
built for. The Makefile also passes `-Werror`, so any declaration that goes
missing becomes a build failure rather than a warning.

`gnu11` is the same C11 language with those declarations restored, which is
what PostgreSQL itself compiles with.
---
 Makefile | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile
index 240a5dc..5517b07 100644
--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
 # the `-Wno`s quiet C90 warnings
-PG_CFLAGS = -std=c11 -Wextra -Wall -Werror \
+PG_CFLAGS = -std=gnu11 -Wextra -Wall -Werror \
 	-Wold-style-definition \
 	-Wno-declaration-after-statement \
 	-Wno-vla \
